course: biology
lesson: 249
title: Defences: tricks and myths: playing dead, dropping tails, ink, and do ostriches bury their heads?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some animals escape not with spikes or poison but with a trick, like a magician who makes you look the wrong way.
explain: A lizard leaves its wriggling tail behind for the cat, a squid vanishes in a cloud of ink, a moth flashes two big fake eyes, and an opossum lies so still and smelly that it seems dead.
explain: But cartoons have spread tricks that animals never do: ostriches do not bury their heads, a chameleon cannot turn tartan, and a camel's hump is not a water tank.
explain: The cartoon ostrich comes from a real sight: an ostrich lowers its head to turn its eggs in a ground nest or lies flat to hide, and from far away it looks headless.
explain: Good science means checking the story against what real animals do.
fact: Ostriches do not bury their heads in sand; they lower their heads to turn eggs in their ground nest or lie flat to hide, and an ostrich can run at about 70 kilometres an hour and kick hard enough to hurt a lion.
fact: A terrified Virginia opossum «plays dead»: it goes stiff, drools and gives off a smell of death, and many predators lose interest, because a dead animal may be rotten; it does not choose this, it is an automatic faint-like reaction that can last hours.
fact: Many lizards can drop their tails at a special breaking point, the wriggling tail keeps the predator busy, and the new tail grows back with cartilage instead of bone and may look different.
fact: Squids and octopuses squirt ink to hide or to make a blob shaped like a fake squid, the ink can dull a predator's senses of smell and taste, and people use squid and cuttlefish ink for writing and in black pasta.
fact: A pufferfish swallows water to blow up to several times its size, which makes it hard to swallow, but this is tiring and stressful, so it does not do it for fun.
fact: Big eyespots on a moth's wings can startle a bird, and small eyespots near the wing tips draw pecks away from the head; butterflies with a torn eyespot often survive.
fact: Chameleons change colour mostly to show mood and temperature, not to match everything around them; tiny crystals in their skin change spacing, which changes the colour of reflected light.
fact: Stick insects look like twigs and sway like them in the wind, so birds hunting by sight miss them; the longest insect known is a stick insect from China, over 60 centimetres with its legs stretched out.
fact: Goldfish can remember things for months: they learn to push levers, come to the sound of feeding and recognise colours, shapes and even music, so the three-second memory is a myth.
fact: Bulls cannot tell red from green: they charge the moving cape, not its colour, and a blue cape waved the same way would work too; never go into a field with a bull.
fact: Most birds have a weak sense of smell and will not abandon a chick because a person touched it; the real danger is moving the chick away from where its parents feed it, and a fluffy bird hopping on the ground is often a fledgling whose parents are near.
fact: A camel's hump stores fat, not water, which gives energy when food is short, and the hump sags as the fat is used up; camels save water by hardly sweating and making dry dung, and can drink up to 100 litres at once.
exercise: Why does an ostrich lower its head to the ground? | To turn its eggs or to lie flat and hide | To bury its head in the sand whenever it is scared | To listen for worms underground | To drink from the sand
exercise: How fast can an ostrich run? | About 70 kilometres an hour | Only about 7 kilometres an hour | About 700 kilometres an hour | It cannot run at all, only waddle
exercise: Does an opossum choose to play dead? | No, it is an automatic faint-like reaction | Yes, it decides to act dead, just like an actor on a stage | Yes, but only for fun | It never plays dead
exercise: Why does playing dead often work? | Many predators avoid dead animals that may be rotten | Predators are scared of ghosts | The opossum becomes invisible | Dead animals taste far too sweet for most hunters to like
exercise: Why does a lizard's dropped tail keep wriggling? | To keep the predator busy while the lizard escapes | So that the dropped tail can grow into a brand-new lizard | To call for help from other lizards | Because the lizard is still inside
exercise: What is a lizard's new tail made with instead of bone? | Cartilage | Wood | Shell | Hair
exercise: What can squid ink do to a predator? | Hide the squid and dull the predator's smell and taste | Burn the predator's skin off | Put the predator to sleep for a whole day and night | Turn the predator into a squid
exercise: Why does a pufferfish blow itself up? | To be too big and hard to swallow | To float up to the surface for air | To show off to other fish just for fun | To scare away other pufferfish only
exercise: What do big eyespots on a moth's wings do? | Startle birds that may think they see a big animal's eyes | Help the moth to see what is coming up behind it | Store water for the moth on long hot days in the middle of summer | Keep the moth warm at night
exercise: What do chameleons mostly change colour for? | To show mood and temperature | To match every background exactly | To copy the cartoons on television | To hide from the falling rain
exercise: How does a stick insect avoid being eaten? | It looks like a twig and sways like one in the wind | It bites birds that come near | It runs faster than birds can fly | It glows in the dark at night to scare birds away from the twigs
exercise: How long can a goldfish remember things? | For months | For three seconds | For one minute | Hardly at all
exercise: Why does a bull charge a bullfighter's cape? | Because it moves, not because it is red | Because red makes bulls angry | Because red smells like food | Because it wants to play
exercise: What happens if you touch a baby bird and put it back in its nest? | The parents usually keep caring for it | The mother always abandons it | The mother eats it | The chick turns into a different bird
exercise: A fluffy bird is hopping on the ground and cannot fly well. What is it often? | A fledgling learning to fly, with parents near | A lost baby that must be taken home | A sick bird that will die | A chick that fell from space
exercise: What does a camel's hump store? | Fat | Water | Hot air | Milk
exercise: What happens to a camel's hump when the fat is used up? | It sags | It fills with water | It falls off | It grows bigger
test: In a cartoon an ostrich sticks its head in the sand when a lion comes. What would a real ostrich more likely do? | Run away very fast or kick | Bury its head and wait | Climb a tree | Fly away high
test: In a cartoon a chameleon walks onto a tartan blanket and turns tartan. What is right? | Real chameleons change colour mostly for mood and temperature, and cannot copy patterns like that | Yes, real chameleons can copy any pattern they sit on in a second, even stripes and spots | They turn invisible on blankets | They only change colour at night
test: In a cartoon Bugs Bunny waves a red cape and the bull goes mad at the colour. What is true? | The bull charges the moving cape, it cannot tell red from green | Red is the only colour that bulls can see, so it makes them angry | Bulls never charge anything | The bull is chasing the smell of the cape
test: «goldfish only remember 3 seconds, so he won't mind the tiny bowl» What is right? | Goldfish remember for months and can learn tricks, so they need space and interest | True, so a small bare bowl is fine, because it will forget everything every few seconds | True, but only for orange ones | They remember for exactly one day
test: In a cartoon like Tom and Jerry, a baby bird falls out of its nest, the cat touches it, and the mother flies away for good. What is true? | Most birds will not abandon a chick because it was touched | The mother always leaves for ever if a person has touched it | The mother can smell the cat for years | The chick must be raised by the cat
test: «camel's got 2 humps so it's got double water innit» What is right? | No, humps store fat, not water | Yes, each hump is a water tank | Yes, one water and one milk | No, humps are full of air
test: «2 camels with 1 hump each, 3 camels with 2 humps. humps total» | 8 | 5 | 7 | 10
test: «3 ostriches in the field. how many legs? and don't say 12» | 6 | 12 | 9 | 3
test: «4 lizards lost their tails, no sorry 3 lost them. how many still got tails if there's 7 lizards» | 4 | 3 | 7 | 5
test: What would happen if a hungry bird pecked the eyespot near the tip of a butterfly's wing? | The wing would tear, but the butterfly could escape alive | The butterfly would die at once | The bird would be blinded | The eyespot would bite back
test: «the possum's dead, look, tongue out and stinks. chuck it in the bin?» What is right? | Leave it alone, it may be playing dead and will get up later | It is certainly dead | It is asleep and just wants some food | It is pretending to be dead so that it can jump up and bite you
test: «why'd the squid leave a fake squid shape in the water lol» | To fool the predator into attacking the ink | It is drawing pictures for fun | It is leaving a message for friends | It is throwing up its dinner
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct popularly described as an ostrich burying its head in the sand? | It is a myth arising from the lowering of the head to tend eggs or to lie concealed | It is a well-documented response to the approach of predators, observed by many naturalists in Africa | It is practised exclusively by juvenile ostriches | It is a means of obtaining water from beneath the sand
exam: Identify the principal substance stored within the hump of a camel. | Fat, which furnishes energy when food is scarce | Water, which is released when the animal is thirsty | Air, which assists the animal in floating | Milk, which is reserved for the young
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the response of a bull to the cape of a bullfighter. | The animal responds to the movement of the cape rather than to its colour | The animal is enraged specifically by the colour red, which it perceives more vividly than any other | The animal perceives red as its sole visible colour | The animal is attracted by the odour of the dye
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the shedding of the tail benefits a lizard pursued by a predator. | The wriggling tail engages the attention of the predator whilst the lizard escapes | The tail is venomous and poisons the predator | The lizard thereby becomes invisible to its attacker | The tail summons other lizards to its assistance, which then drive the predator away together
